GOLD FACT TABLE
Fact_sales
sale_id,product_id,customer_id,store_id,sale_date,quantity
CREATE TABLE fact_sales (
  sale_id INT,
  customer_id INT,
  product_id INT,
  store_id INT,
  date_key DATE,
  quantity INT,
  total_amount INT   —- total_amount= quantity * unit_price
)
PARTITIONED BY (date_key)
STORED AS PARQUET;

PySpark Fact Load
from pyspark.sql.functions import col

def load_fact_sales(spark):
    sales = spark.table("silver_sales")
    products = spark.table("silver_products")

    fact_df = (
        sales.join(products, "product_id")
             .withColumn("total_amount", col("quantity") * col("unit_price"))
             .select(
                 "sale_id",
                 "customer_id",
                 "product_id",
                 "store_id",
                 col("sale_date").alias("date_key"),
                 "quantity",
                 "total_amount"
             )
    )

    (
        fact_df.write
        .mode("overwrite")
        .partitionBy("date_key")
        .format("parquet")
        .saveAsTable("fact_sales")
    )

Example Business Queries (Hive)
Total Revenue by Product
SELECT p.product_name, SUM(f.total_amount) AS revenue
FROM fact_sales f
JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_id = p.product_id
GROUP BY p.product_name;

Sales by Store
SELECT s.store_name, SUM(f.total_amount) AS revenue
FROM fact_sales f
JOIN dim_store s ON f.store_id = s.store_id
GROUP BY s.store_name;

Interview Talking Points
Why Parquet?
Columnar, compressed, faster analytics.
Why Medallion Architecture?
Separation of concerns, scalability, auditability.
Why Star Schema in Hive?
Optimized joins, BI-friendly, fast aggregations.
Why partition fact by date?
Most queries are time-based → partition pruning.







